[13] My review of the Guideline informs me as follows:
(i) the evidence must show that what the claimant genuinely fears is persecution on a Convention ground, as opposed to random acts perpetrated against her as an individual;
(ii) gender-related claims cannot be rejected simply because a claimant comes from a country where women face generalized oppression, and her fear of persecution is not individually identifiable to her;
(iii) decision-makers should consider evidence indicating a failure of state protection if the state in the country of origin is unwilling or unable to provide adequate protection from gender-related persecution;
(iv) in determining whether the state is willing or able to provide state protection, decision-makers should consider the fact that the forms of evidence which a claimant might consider "clear and convincing" proof of state inability to protect will not always be available or useful in gender related persecution; and
(v) reference may need to be made to alternative forms of evidence to meet the "clear and convincing" test.

