subthemes.text:
[43] Refugee status may be denied to persons who, despite having a well-founded fear of persecution on a Convention ground remain able to avail themselves of the protection of their country of origin.42 In Magonza, the Federal Court summarizes certain fundamental principles of state protection arising from the jurisprudence. They are as follows:
(i) the burden of proving not only a well-founded fear of persecution, but also that their country of nationality is unable or unwilling to protect them is on the refugee claimant;
(ii) the proper test for state protection is that it must be effective at the operational level;
(iii) generally, inadequate state protection is a systemic, not an individual issue and for that reason, "country evidence may be more useful than evidence concerning the applicant's situation in determining whether state protection is adequate"; and
(iv) assessing a country's "level of democracy" may or may not be related to a country's capacity to offer adequate state protection. The issue of state protection does not arise only in so-called "failed states." 43

[44] As indicated above, it is insufficient to establish that a state is making its "best efforts" or is attempting to make improvements to its domestic situation with respect to the adequacy of state protection. The appropriate test is the test of operational adequacy which involves an assessment as to whether state protection is adequate at the operational level.

